// Golden set — one JSON object per line. Lines starting with // are ignored.
//
// 50 questions. The first 20 were the lesson target and are the set every result
// published before 9 Sep 2026 was measured on; the last 30 were added 9 Sep 2026 and
// are marked below. The point still stands: this is
// YOUR opinion about YOUR corpus. Read every keyword line and make sure you'd
// defend it. Deciding what counts as a right answer is the part of this loop a
// machine can't do for you, and it's the part an interviewer is actually testing.
//
// Fields:
//   question          what you'd actually ask the system
//   keywords          strings that must appear in a chunk for it to count as
//                     relevant. This is the whole ballgame — see README.
//   reference_answer  the true answer, in your words. Not scored by this
//                     harness (no LLM judge here), but write it anyway: if you
//                     can't, the question is ambiguous and will poison the set.
//                     These answers are worded from general knowledge, NOT
//                     lifted from the corpus — an independent ground truth.
//   category          direct_fact | concept | spanning — lets you slice the
//                     results later and find WHICH kind of question fails.
//
// Picking keywords, learned the hard way:
//   - Too generic ("RAG", "model") and every chunk matches. MRR pins to ~1.0
//     and the experiment is dead — you've measured nothing.
//   - Too many, or spread across paragraphs, and no chunk holds them all.
//     Automatic zero, forever, no matter how good retrieval gets.
//   - Aim for 2-3 terms that co-occur in the passage a human would point at.
//   - Run `check` after every batch you write. It catches both failures.

// ---- Seed examples (worked for you in the original scaffold) ----
{"question": "What does NDCG measure that MRR does not?", "keywords": ["NDCG", "scattered"], "reference_answer": "MRR only looks at the position of the first relevant chunk. NDCG considers all relevant chunks, asking whether they are near the top or scattered throughout the results.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "How is IoU calculated for a predicted bounding box?", "keywords": ["IoU", "Area of Overlap", "Area of Union"], "reference_answer": "IoU = Area of Overlap / Area of Union — the overlap between predicted and ground-truth boxes divided by their combined area.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What does LoRA stand for?", "keywords": ["LoRA", "Low Rank Adapters"], "reference_answer": "Low Rank Adapters — the technique that makes modern fine-tuning practical and affordable.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Which tool in the LangChain ecosystem handles monitoring and debugging?", "keywords": ["LangSmith", "monitoring", "debugging"], "reference_answer": "LangSmith is the monitoring and observability tool for the LangChain ecosystem, providing visibility into LLM calls, traces, and cost tracking. It works with both LangChain and LangGraph.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Why is RAG optimization described as whack-a-mole?", "keywords": ["whack-a-mole", "chunking"], "reference_answer": "Because fixing one failing question by adjusting chunking causes a different question to start failing. Improvements are not monotonic, which is why you need a golden set and a metric rather than reasoning from first principles.", "category": "concept"}

// ---- Your five ----
{"question": "What is the Chat Completions API?", "keywords": ["chat", "completion", "interact", "LLMs"], "reference_answer": "The Chat Completions API is the standard, widely-adopted interface for sending messages to an LLM and getting a response back. OpenAI introduced it and most other providers adopted the same format.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "How are chat models different from reasoning models?", "keywords": ["conversation", "think", "answer"], "reference_answer": "Chat models are trained specifically for conversation and answer directly. Reasoning models think before answering, working through the problem step by step and often leaving a visible thinking trace.", "category": "spanning"}
{"question": "How are LangChain and LiteLLM different?", "keywords": ["heavyweight", "lightweight"], "reference_answer": "LangChain is a heavyweight, feature-rich and powerful framework with a steep learning curve. LiteLLM is lightweight and easy to use, with minimal abstraction.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What is prompt caching?", "keywords": ["prompt caching", "reuse", "processing"], "reference_answer": "Prompt caching saves the processing work for a frequently repeated prompt so the provider reuses it instead of recomputing from scratch each time, which saves money and latency.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What is computer vision?", "keywords": ["interpret", "understand", "visual information"], "reference_answer": "Computer vision is a branch of AI that trains machines to interpret and understand the visual world — extracting meaning from images, video, and camera feeds.", "category": "direct_fact"}

// ---- Ten new, drawn from across the corpus (Day 11-31) ----
{"question": "How does RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter differ from a plain character splitter?", "keywords": ["RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter", "natural boundaries"], "reference_answer": "It splits text hierarchically at natural boundaries — trying paragraph breaks first, then line breaks, then sentences, then words — so chunks stay coherent. A plain character splitter just cuts at a fixed length regardless of structure.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "How does an LSTM solve the vanishing gradient problem?", "keywords": ["LSTM", "vanishing gradient", "cell state"], "reference_answer": "An LSTM carries information along a gated cell state — a memory highway that runs across time steps with minimal change — regulated by forget, input, and output gates. Because gradients flow along that path largely unchanged, they don't shrink to zero over long sequences.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What problem do skip connections in ResNet solve?", "keywords": ["skip connection", "ResNet"], "reference_answer": "Skip (residual) connections let a layer's input bypass layers and add directly to their output, giving gradients a direct path backward. This lets very deep networks train without the signal degrading or vanishing.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What does mAP measure in object detection?", "keywords": ["mAP", "Mean Average Precision"], "reference_answer": "mAP (mean Average Precision) summarizes detection quality by averaging the average-precision score across all classes, capturing both how accurately and how completely a model detects objects.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What does reranking do in a RAG pipeline?", "keywords": ["Reranking", "reorder", "relevance"], "reference_answer": "Reranking is a second pass that reorders an initially retrieved set of chunks by relevance to the query — often with a stronger model — so the most relevant passages rise to the top before being sent to the generator.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What is Gradio used for?", "keywords": ["Gradio", "web interface", "Python"], "reference_answer": "Gradio is an open-source Python library for quickly building shareable web interfaces for machine-learning models and demos, with no frontend code required.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What is Async IO in Python?", "keywords": ["Async IO", "multithreading", "multiprocessing"], "reference_answer": "Async IO is Python's single-threaded concurrency model built on an event loop, letting a program interleave many I/O-bound tasks while they wait — a lightweight alternative to multithreading or multiprocessing.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What is overfitting in a neural network?", "keywords": ["Overfitting", "Training accuracy", "Validation accuracy"], "reference_answer": "Overfitting is when a model learns the training data too closely, including its noise, so it generalizes poorly — visible when training accuracy is much higher than validation accuracy.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What is backpropagation?", "keywords": ["Backward propagation", "weights", "minimize"], "reference_answer": "Backpropagation is the training algorithm that computes how much each weight contributed to the error and updates the weights, via gradient descent, to minimize that error.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What is Non-Maximum Suppression in object detection?", "keywords": ["Non-Maximum Suppression", "duplicate"], "reference_answer": "Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS) is a post-processing step that removes duplicate overlapping boxes for the same object, keeping only the highest-confidence detection.", "category": "direct_fact"}

// ---- Added 9 Sep 2026: questions 21-50 ----
//
// Why: the original 20 clustered on the files I happened to have worked in, and left
// ten of the twenty-one corpus documents with no question at all (11, 14, 17, 18, 19,
// 20, 21, 23, 29, 30). A golden set that never asks about a document cannot tell you
// whether retrieval works on it. These 30 were written against those gaps first.
//
// Method, so it can be argued with: for each question I read the passage a human would
// point at, wrote the reference answer from general knowledge WITHOUT the file open,
// then chose keywords that co-occur in that passage and ran `check` until every one
// landed inside the 1-25 chunk band. Nine candidate keyword sets were rejected on the
// first pass and rewritten; three questions were rewritten entirely because no keyword
// set could be made both specific and hittable.
//
// IMPORTANT - what this does to published numbers: every figure in the reports dated
// before 9 Sep 2026 was measured on the first 20 questions. Those numbers are NOT
// re-stated against 50. Any figure without an explicit n is n=20. The n=50 sweep is a
// separate, dated run.

{"question": "What does Ollama let you do that a cloud LLM service does not?", "keywords": ["Ollama", "open-source", "personal computer"], "reference_answer": "Run open-weight models entirely on your own machine, so prompts never leave it, there is no per-token cost, and you are not limited to the models a provider chooses to host. The internet is only needed to download the model.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What counts against the context window besides the new message?", "keywords": ["context window", "System prompt", "conversation history"], "reference_answer": "The system prompt, the whole conversation history so far, the new message, and the response the model is about to generate all share the same token budget.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What does the Chinchilla Scaling Law say about parameters and training data?", "keywords": ["Chinchilla", "parameters", "training data"], "reference_answer": "Parameters and training tokens should scale together. Double the parameters and you need roughly double the training data before the extra parameters earn their keep.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "Why is the GPQA benchmark called Google-proof?", "keywords": ["Google-Proof", "physics, chemistry"], "reference_answer": "Its physics, chemistry and biology questions cannot be answered by looking things up. Non-experts given Google and half an hour score about 34 percent, so the benchmark measures understanding rather than search skill.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "Why was MMLU Pro created to replace the original MMLU?", "keywords": ["MMLU Pro", "10 answer choices", "saturating"], "reference_answer": "The original had gone stale: models were saturating near the ceiling and some items were ambiguous. MMLU Pro widens each question to ten answer choices and removes the ambiguity, so it discriminates between strong models again.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What is a vector embedding?", "keywords": ["vector embedding", "list of numbers", "meaning"], "reference_answer": "A list of numbers that encodes the meaning of a piece of text, arranged so that texts meaning similar things land close together in that number space.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Why does overlap between chunks matter?", "keywords": ["Overlap", "complete context"], "reference_answer": "A hard split can cut a sentence or an idea in half, leaving neither chunk able to answer. Repeating a slice of text across the boundary means at least one chunk carries the complete thought.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "Which matters more for semantic search quality, the encoder or the vector database?", "keywords": ["Encoder", "Vector Database", "dimensionality"], "reference_answer": "The encoder. It creates the vectors and sets their dimensionality, so it decides how good the semantic matching is. The database only stores and retrieves them, which is an infrastructure choice.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "How is MRR scored when the first relevant chunk is third in the results?", "keywords": ["Mean Reciprocal Rank", "1/3", "0.33"], "reference_answer": "One over the rank of the first relevant hit, so 1/3, about 0.33. Those per-question scores are averaged across the test set to give MRR.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What three dimensions does an LLM judge score an answer on?", "keywords": ["Accuracy", "Completeness", "Relevance"], "reference_answer": "Accuracy, whether the facts are right. Completeness, whether anything needed is missing. Relevance, whether it padded the answer with things nobody asked for.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Why does precision matter less than recall in RAG?", "keywords": ["Precision", "recall", "ignoring irrelevant"], "reference_answer": "Models are fairly good at ignoring irrelevant passages, so a few junk chunks rarely spoil the answer, whereas a missing chunk makes the answer impossible. Precision starts to matter when context cost is tight or the noise is actively misleading.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "Which of the agentic hallmarks is treated as the litmus test?", "keywords": ["Tool Use", "litmus test"], "reference_answer": "Tool use. Once a model can call a function and act on the world rather than only emit text, most people will call the system agentic.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "How does Anthropic distinguish a workflow from an agent?", "keywords": ["Workflows", "predefined paths", "dynamically direct"], "reference_answer": "A workflow routes models and tools along paths you defined in advance. An agent directs its own process, choosing the steps as it goes. Both are agentic, so it is a spectrum rather than a binary.", "category": "spanning"}
{"question": "What is the evaluator-optimizer pattern?", "keywords": ["Evaluator-Optimizer", "feedback loop", "Generator"], "reference_answer": "One model generates, a second model judges, and rejected output goes back to the generator with feedback. The loop repeats until the evaluator accepts or an attempt limit is hit.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What are the three types of guardrail?", "keywords": ["Input Guardrails", "Process Guardrails", "Output Guardrails"], "reference_answer": "Input guardrails validate the request before anything runs. Process guardrails constrain the run itself, capping iterations, restricting tools, requiring approval. Output guardrails check what comes back before the user sees it.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Why is tool calling described as a conjuring trick?", "keywords": ["conjuring trick", "Tool Descriptions"], "reference_answer": "The model never reaches into your machine. You send it tool descriptions, it replies asking for one, your own code notices that, runs the function and sends the result back in a second call. The magic is ordinary control flow.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What does the word resources mean when improving an LLM's answers?", "keywords": ["Resources", "more context", "expertise"], "reference_answer": "Putting the information the model lacks straight into the prompt so it can answer from specifics rather than generalities. Mechanically it is just extra text in the request.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What happens if you call a coroutine without awaiting it?", "keywords": ["coroutine object", "does NOT execute"], "reference_answer": "Nothing runs. You get a coroutine object back. The body only executes once it is scheduled, normally by awaiting it.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What is a handoff in the OpenAI Agents SDK?", "keywords": ["Handoff", "delegates control", "relay race"], "reference_answer": "One agent passing control to another and not getting it back, like a baton in a relay. The receiving agent owns the rest of the run.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "Why are Pydantic models used for structured outputs?", "keywords": ["Pydantic", "BaseModel", "type hints"], "reference_answer": "A class inheriting BaseModel with type hints converts to a JSON schema that constrains generation, and the reply is validated back into a typed Python object. Field docstrings go to the model as guidance.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Where in a workflow can guardrails be placed?", "keywords": ["Guardrails", "system boundaries", "middle of workflows"], "reference_answer": "Only at the system boundaries: before the first agent sees input, and after the last one produces output. You cannot drop one into the middle of a workflow.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Why does LangGraph keep state immutable?", "keywords": ["Immutable", "Time Travel", "Checkpointing"], "reference_answer": "Because old states stay valid, you can checkpoint anywhere, rewind and replay, trace exactly how a run evolved, and let parallel nodes work without clobbering each other.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What problem do reducers solve in LangGraph?", "keywords": ["reducer", "parallel", "overwrite"], "reference_answer": "Two nodes finishing in parallel both return a new value for the same field, and a plain merge would let one overwrite the other. A reducer says how to combine them instead, so nothing is lost.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What is quantization in the context of model weights?", "keywords": ["Quantization", "precision", "model weights"], "reference_answer": "Storing weights at lower numeric precision so the model needs far less memory. Fewer bits means fewer distinct values each weight can take, which is the accuracy you trade away.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "What makes NF4 better than naive 4-bit quantization?", "keywords": ["NF4", "normal distribution", "16 values"], "reference_answer": "Its 16 available values are spaced to match the normal distribution that neural network weights actually follow, so the same four bits lose less information than an evenly spaced scheme.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Which projections are targeted first when applying LoRA adapters?", "keywords": ["q_proj", "k_proj", "v_proj"], "reference_answer": "The attention projections, q_proj, k_proj, v_proj and o_proj. They decide what the model attends to, so adapting them buys the most behaviour change per trainable parameter. MLP layers are added only if that is not enough.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What is transfer learning and why does it work?", "keywords": ["Transfer learning", "ImageNet", "generic features"], "reference_answer": "Reusing a network trained on a big dataset such as ImageNet for a different task. The early layers have already learned generic edges, textures and colours that almost any image task needs, so only the final layers need retraining.", "category": "concept"}
{"question": "How does Otsu's method pick a threshold?", "keywords": ["Otsu", "histogram", "automatically finds"], "reference_answer": "It reads the image histogram and searches for the cut that minimises the spread within the two resulting pixel groups. It works when the histogram is clearly bimodal and struggles under uneven lighting.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What is hysteresis thresholding in the Canny edge detector?", "keywords": ["Hysteresis Thresholding", "High threshold", "sure edges"], "reference_answer": "Canny uses two thresholds instead of one. Pixels above the high one are sure edges, pixels below the low one are discarded, and the ones in between survive only if they connect to a sure edge. That is what keeps faint real boundaries while dropping isolated noise.", "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "How does background subtraction differ from frame differencing?", "keywords": ["Background subtraction", "frame differencing", "statistical model"], "reference_answer": "Frame differencing only compares one frame to the next, so it sees instantaneous change. Background subtraction keeps a statistical model of the scene and flags whatever fails to match it, which survives gradual lighting shifts and catches objects that have stopped moving.", "category": "spanning"}
